IMPORT prelude

-- Administration Act 1903 (Western Australia) -- who takes on an intestacy and how much (s 14 and its Table, s 14A
-- legacy sums, s 15 de facto partners, ss 12A-12B relationships, s 13(1a) land, the Fourth Schedule election to take the
-- home), and what a grant is and does for the person who must take it out (ss 6-8, 25, 32, 33, 36, 37, 42, 43-44, 55,
-- 71, 139, 143A).
--
-- SEAT: the heir or next of kin of a person who has died (spouse, de facto partner, child, parent, brother or sister,
-- grandparent, uncle or aunt), and the executor or would-be administrator of the estate. Every decision is phrased as one
-- of them would ask it: "How much do I take", "Which grant must I apply for", "May I be granted administration".
--
-- Provisions covered: s 6, s 7, s 8, s 12A, s 12B (as a counting rule), s 13(1a), s 14(1) and the Table items 1-11,
-- s 14(1A), (1B), (2), (2a)-(2b), (3), (3a), (4), s 14A(1), s 15(1)-(3) and (5), s 25, s 32, s 33(1), s 36, s 37, s 42,
-- s 43(1)(b) with s 44, s 55, s 71, s 139(1)(a), s 143A, Part V (as the absence of death duty), Fourth Schedule cll 1-3.
--
-- Edition: the compilation incorporating amendments up to the Administration Amendment Act 2022 (No. 5 of 2022, in
-- operation 30 March 2022), last reprint listed Reprint 12 as at 15 April 2016, as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_49096.txt (retrieved 7 September 2026). No subsidiary instrument is used: the
-- Administration (Intestacy Legacy Amounts) Orders 2023 and 2025 made under s 14A (named in the deposit's note 8, figures
-- not given) and the Non-Contentious Probate Rules 1967 are NOT deposited, so the legacy sums, the s 139 ceiling and the
-- rules' period for accounts are INPUTS. `the original sums` is what s 14A(1) states.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: s 14(5) income, s 14(7) partition, s 15(4) two or more de facto partners, s 17 and s 17A (infants'
-- shares), s 10A and the Fifth Schedule (insolvent estates), s 26 sureties, s 29 revocation, ss 34-35 and 38-41 (attorneys,
-- receivers, special letters), s 45-47A, Part III (resealing), Part IV (caveats), Fourth Schedule cll 4-8 (how the
-- election is made and valued). Machinery out: ss 1-5, 14B (the Minister's review), 54, 56-60, 140-142, 144. Left to
-- other Acts: the Family Provision Act 1972 (preserved by s 60A), survivorship (Transfer of Land Act 1893), the validity
-- of a will (Wills Act 1970), superannuation death benefits, and the Public Trustee Act 1941. Interest under s 14(4) is
-- simple interest on the whole sum unpaid for the time given.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill, from the seat of the
-- individual to whom the law is applied, with the design defaults ruled on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Administration Act 1903 -- the heir's and the executor's questions`

§§ `s 14(1A), (1B), 14A(1) -- the legacy sums in the Table`

-- s 14A(1): "original sum, for a specified item, means -- (a) in the case of item 2 -- $472 000; (b) in the case of item
-- 3(a) and (b) -- $705 000; (c) in the case of item 3(b)(i) -- $56 500; (d) in the case of item 6 -- $56 500".
-- s 14(1B): the declared sum that applies is the one "under the order that has effect at the time the intestate dies".
-- s 14A(3): a declared sum is the original sum indexed to average weekly earnings, "rounded up to the nearest $500".
DECLARE `Legacy sums` HAS
    `item 2 sum`       IS A NUMBER   -- the partner's statutory legacy where the deceased left issue
    `item 3 sum`       IS A NUMBER   -- the partner's statutory legacy where there is no issue but a parent or sibling
    `item 3(b)(i) sum` IS A NUMBER   -- the parents' first slice of the other half under item 3
    `item 6 sum`       IS A NUMBER   -- the parents' first slice where no partner and no issue survive

`the original sums` MEANS `Legacy sums` WITH
    `item 2 sum`       IS 472000
    `item 3 sum`       IS 705000
    `item 3(b)(i) sum` IS 56500
    `item 6 sum`       IS 56500

§§ `s 14 Table, s 15, s 12B -- the family the deceased left`

-- The facts an heir can state about who survived. "Issue" (s 14) are descendants; a "line" is a child who survived, or a
-- child who died first leaving issue who survived (s 14(2b)(b)). The same for brothers and sisters (s 14(3a)(b)) and,
-- under item 10, for uncles and aunts. s 12B: "it is immaterial whether a relationship is of the whole blood or of the
-- half blood", so half-brothers and half-sisters are counted with the rest.
DECLARE `The family left behind` HAS
    `a husband or wife survived`                                           IS A BOOLEAN
    `years lived with a de facto partner immediately before the death`     IS A NUMBER   -- 0 if no de facto partner
    `lived as husband or wife of the spouse during any part of that time`  IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 15(2)(b), s 15(3)(b)
    `children who survived`                                                IS A NUMBER
    `children who died first leaving issue who survived`                   IS A NUMBER
    `parents who survived`                                                 IS A NUMBER
    `brothers and sisters who survived`                                    IS A NUMBER
    `brothers and sisters who died first leaving children who survived`    IS A NUMBER
    `grandparents who survived`                                            IS A NUMBER
    `uncles and aunts who survived`                                        IS A NUMBER
    `uncles and aunts who died first leaving children who survived`        IS A NUMBER

-- Who is asking. s 15(5): an entitled de facto partner "is to be taken to be a husband or wife" for s 14.
DECLARE `My place in the family` IS ONE OF
    `the surviving husband or wife`
    `the de facto partner`
    `a child, or the issue of a child who died first, between them`
    `a parent`
    `a brother or sister, or the children of one who died first, between them`
    `a grandparent`
    `an uncle or aunt, or the children of one who died first, between them`
    `nobody the Table names`

§§ `s 15 -- de facto partners`

-- s 15(1): no husband or wife, and a de facto partner who lived with the intestate "for a period of at least 2 years
-- immediately before the death" takes what a husband or wife would have taken.
-- s 15(2): a husband or wife AND such a de facto partner, where the intestate "did not, during the whole or any part of that
-- period, live as the husband or wife of the person to whom he or she was married": the de facto partner takes "one-half
-- of the intestate property to which the husband or wife would have been entitled" and the spouse the other half.
-- s 15(3): the same, but "at least 5 years": the de facto partner takes it all.
@export Does the deceased's de facto partner take under s 15 of the Administration Act 1903?
GIVEN `the family` IS A `The family left behind` @desc Who survived the deceased
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does a de facto partner take` IF
        `the family`'s `years lived with a de facto partner immediately before the death` AT LEAST 2
    AND (    NOT `the family`'s `a husband or wife survived`
          OR NOT `the family`'s `lived as husband or wife of the spouse during any part of that time`)

@export What fraction of the partner's entitlement on intestacy goes to the de facto partner (s 15)?
GIVEN `the family` IS A `The family left behind` @desc Who survived the deceased
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the de facto partner's fraction` IS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `Does a de facto partner take` `the family`                                                   THEN 0
        IF NOT `the family`'s `a husband or wife survived`                                                   THEN 1
        IF `the family`'s `years lived with a de facto partner immediately before the death` AT LEAST 5      THEN 1
        OTHERWISE                                                                                                 1 DIVIDED BY 2

@export Does anyone take as the deceased's partner (husband, wife or qualifying de facto partner) under the s 14 Table?
GIVEN `the family` IS A `The family left behind` @desc Who survived the deceased
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does a partner take` IF
    `the family`'s `a husband or wife survived` OR `Does a de facto partner take` `the family`

§§ `s 14(1) Table -- which item applies`

-- Items: 1 a husband or wife takes "all household chattels"; 2 "a husband or wife and issue"; 3 "a husband or wife and one
-- or more of ... a parent, a brother or sister, or child of a brother or sister, but leaving no issue"; 4 a husband or
-- wife only; 5 "issue but no husband or wife"; 6 parent(s) and a brother, sister or child of one; 7 parent(s) only; 8
-- brothers, sisters or their children only; 9 "a grandparent or grandparents"; 10 "an uncle or aunt or a child of an
-- uncle or aunt"; 11 none of these: "the whole of the intestate property passes to the Crown by way of escheat". Item 1
-- runs alongside items 2 to 4; the item returned here is the one that divides the rest.
DECLARE `Table item` IS ONE OF
    `item 2, a partner and issue`
    `item 3, a partner and a parent or sibling line, no issue`
    `item 4, a partner alone`
    `item 5, issue and no partner`
    `item 6, a parent and a sibling line`
    `item 7, a parent alone`
    `item 8, sibling lines alone`
    `item 9, grandparents`
    `item 10, uncles and aunts or their children`
    `item 11, nobody, so the Crown`

@export How many lines of issue does the deceased leave (children who survived, plus children who died first leaving issue who survived)?
GIVEN `the family` IS A `The family left behind` @desc Who survived the deceased
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the lines of issue` IS
    `the family`'s `children who survived` PLUS `the family`'s `children who died first leaving issue who survived`

@export How many brother-and-sister lines does the deceased leave (s 14(3a))?
GIVEN `the family` IS A `The family left behind` @desc Who survived the deceased
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the sibling lines` IS
    `the family`'s `brothers and sisters who survived` PLUS `the family`'s `brothers and sisters who died first leaving children who survived`

@export How many uncle-and-aunt lines does the deceased leave (item 10 with s 14(3a))?
GIVEN `the family` IS A `The family left behind` @desc Who survived the deceased
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the uncle and aunt lines` IS
    `the family`'s `uncles and aunts who survived` PLUS `the family`'s `uncles and aunts who died first leaving children who survived`

@export Which item of the Table in s 14(1) of the Administration Act 1903 divides my relative's intestate property?
GIVEN `the family` IS A `The family left behind` @desc Who survived the deceased
GIVETH A `Table item`
DECIDE `Which item of the Table applies` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `Does a partner take` `the family` AND `the lines of issue` `the family` GREATER THAN 0             THEN `item 2, a partner and issue`
        IF `Does a partner take` `the family`
           AND (`the family`'s `parents who survived` GREATER THAN 0 OR `the sibling lines` `the family` GREATER THAN 0)
                                                                                                             THEN `item 3, a partner and a parent or sibling line, no issue`
        IF `Does a partner take` `the family`                                                                THEN `item 4, a partner alone`
        IF `the lines of issue` `the family` GREATER THAN 0                                                  THEN `item 5, issue and no partner`
        IF `the family`'s `parents who survived` GREATER THAN 0 AND `the sibling lines` `the family` GREATER THAN 0
                                                                                                             THEN `item 6, a parent and a sibling line`
        IF `the family`'s `parents who survived` GREATER THAN 0                                              THEN `item 7, a parent alone`
        IF `the sibling lines` `the family` GREATER THAN 0                                                   THEN `item 8, sibling lines alone`
        IF `the family`'s `grandparents who survived` GREATER THAN 0                                         THEN `item 9, grandparents`
        IF `the uncle and aunt lines` `the family` GREATER THAN 0                                            THEN `item 10, uncles and aunts or their children`
        OTHERWISE                                                                                                 `item 11, nobody, so the Crown`

@export Does my relative's intestate property pass to the Crown by escheat (Table item 11)?
GIVEN `the family` IS A `The family left behind` @desc Who survived the deceased
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the property pass to the Crown` IF
    `Which item of the Table applies` `the family` EQUALS `item 11, nobody, so the Crown`

§§ `s 14(1) -- only the intestate property`

-- s 14(1): the Table distributes "the property as to which they die intestate (in this section called the intestate
-- property)". A will that disposes of everything leaves nothing for the Table.
@export After the will, how much of my relative's net estate is left to be divided as intestate property under s 14?
GIVEN `the net estate` IS A NUMBER @desc The net value of the estate at the date of death, in dollars
      `the value the will disposes of` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of the property the will effectively gives away, in dollars (0 if there is no will)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How much is left for the Table` IS
    IF `the value the will disposes of` AT LEAST `the net estate` THEN 0 ELSE `the net estate` MINUS `the value the will disposes of`

§§ `s 14(1) Table items 2 to 4, s 14(3) -- the partner's entitlement`

-- Item 2(a): where the net value (other than household chattels) "does not exceed the sum of $472 000 (or ... that sum)"
-- the husband or wife takes "the whole of the intestate property". (b): above it, the husband or wife takes that sum
-- "absolutely, together with interest" and "of the residue ... one third and the issue ... the other twothirds".
-- s 14(3): with "(a) a husband or wife and one child; or (b) a husband or wife and the issue of one child", read "onehalf"
-- and "half". Item 3(a): not exceeding $705 000, the whole; (b): above it, $705 000 and "of the residue ... onehalf".
-- Item 4: "the whole of the intestate property". s 14(2)(b): the net value is "the net value of that property at the date
-- of the death of the intestate".
@export How much of my relative's intestate property goes to the partner side (husband, wife or de facto partner, before any s 15 split) under the Table?
GIVEN `the family` IS A `The family left behind` @desc Who survived the deceased
      `the sums` IS A `Legacy sums` @desc The legacy sums in force when the deceased died (the original sums unless a s 14A order applies)
      `the net value` IS A NUMBER @desc Net value of the intestate property at the date of death in dollars, leaving out household chattels
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the partner's entitlement` IS
    CONSIDER `Which item of the Table applies` `the family`
    WHEN `item 2, a partner and issue` THEN
        IF `the net value` AT MOST `the sums`'s `item 2 sum`
        THEN `the net value`
        ELSE `the sums`'s `item 2 sum` PLUS ((`the net value` MINUS `the sums`'s `item 2 sum`) TIMES `the partner's fraction of the residue`)
    WHEN `item 3, a partner and a parent or sibling line, no issue` THEN
        IF `the net value` AT MOST `the sums`'s `item 3 sum`
        THEN `the net value`
        ELSE `the sums`'s `item 3 sum` PLUS ((`the net value` MINUS `the sums`'s `item 3 sum`) DIVIDED BY 2)
    WHEN `item 4, a partner alone` THEN `the net value`
    OTHERWISE 0
    WHERE
        `the partner's fraction of the residue` MEANS
            IF `the lines of issue` `the family` EQUALS 1 THEN 1 DIVIDED BY 2 ELSE 1 DIVIDED BY 3

-- s 14(4): the husband or wife entitled to the item 2 or item 3 sum is also entitled to "an amount equal to 5% per annum on
-- the sum ... or on that part of such sum as remains unpaid or unsatisfied, calculated from the date of death of the
-- intestate to the date of the payment of that sum" (or its appropriation under the Trustees Act 1962, if earlier).
@export How much interest am I owed on my statutory legacy under s 14(4) (5% a year from the death until it is paid)?
GIVEN `the sum unpaid` IS A NUMBER @desc The part of the item 2 or item 3 legacy sum still unpaid, in dollars
      `years from the death to payment` IS A NUMBER @desc Years (may be fractional) from the death to payment or appropriation, whichever is earlier
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How much interest am I owed on my legacy` IS
    `the sum unpaid` TIMES 5 DIVIDED BY 100 TIMES `years from the death to payment`

§§ `s 14(1) Table items 2 and 5, s 14(2a)-(2b) -- the issue`

-- Item 5: "the issue shall be entitled in accordance with subsection (2b) to the whole of the intestate property".
-- s 14(2b)(a): "where a person is the only entitled issue of the intestate, then that person is entitled to all".
-- s 14(2b)(b): otherwise the property is "divided into as many equal parts as there are children of the intestate who --
-- (i) survived the intestate; or (ii) left issue who survived the intestate"; (c) a dead child's part goes to that child's
-- issue, divided among them as if that child had died intestate.
@export How much of my relative's intestate property goes to the issue (children and the issue of dead children) together?
GIVEN `the family` IS A `The family left behind` @desc Who survived the deceased
      `the sums` IS A `Legacy sums` @desc The legacy sums in force when the deceased died
      `the net value` IS A NUMBER @desc Net value of the intestate property at the date of death in dollars, leaving out household chattels where a partner survives
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the issue's share` IS
    CONSIDER `Which item of the Table applies` `the family`
    WHEN `item 2, a partner and issue` THEN `the net value` MINUS `the partner's entitlement` `the family` `the sums` `the net value`
    WHEN `item 5, issue and no partner` THEN `the net value`
    OTHERWISE 0

§§ `s 14(1) Table items 3, 6, 7, 8 -- parents, brothers and sisters`

-- Item 3(b) "as to the other half": (i)(A) if "that other half does not exceed the sum of $56 500 ... or if no brother,
-- sister, or child of a brother or sister survives" the parents take it; (B) otherwise the parents take $56 500 and "of the
-- remainder ... onehalf", the brothers and sisters and children of deceased ones "the other half"; (ii) "where neither
-- parent survives" the brothers and sisters take the other half.
-- Item 6(a): not exceeding $56 500, the parents take the whole; (b): above it, $56 500 and "of the residue ... one half",
-- the brothers and sisters the other half. Item 7: the parents take the whole. Item 8: the brothers and sisters the whole.
-- Parents take "in equal shares where both survive".
@export How much of my relative's intestate property goes to the parents together?
GIVEN `the family` IS A `The family left behind` @desc Who survived the deceased
      `the sums` IS A `Legacy sums` @desc The legacy sums in force when the deceased died
      `the net value` IS A NUMBER @desc Net value of the intestate property at the date of death in dollars, leaving out household chattels where a partner survives
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the parents' share` IS
    IF `the family`'s `parents who survived` EQUALS 0 THEN 0
    ELSE CONSIDER `Which item of the Table applies` `the family`
         WHEN `item 3, a partner and a parent or sibling line, no issue` THEN
             IF `the other half` AT MOST `the sums`'s `item 3(b)(i) sum` OR `the sibling lines` `the family` EQUALS 0
             THEN `the other half`
             ELSE `the sums`'s `item 3(b)(i) sum` PLUS ((`the other half` MINUS `the sums`'s `item 3(b)(i) sum`) DIVIDED BY 2)
         WHEN `item 6, a parent and a sibling line` THEN
             IF `the net value` AT MOST `the sums`'s `item 6 sum`
             THEN `the net value`
             ELSE `the sums`'s `item 6 sum` PLUS ((`the net value` MINUS `the sums`'s `item 6 sum`) DIVIDED BY 2)
         WHEN `item 7, a parent alone` THEN `the net value`
         OTHERWISE 0
    WHERE
        `the other half` MEANS
            IF `the net value` AT MOST `the sums`'s `item 3 sum` THEN 0
            ELSE (`the net value` MINUS `the sums`'s `item 3 sum`) DIVIDED BY 2

@export How much of my relative's intestate property goes to the brothers and sisters (and children of dead ones) together?
GIVEN `the family` IS A `The family left behind` @desc Who survived the deceased
      `the sums` IS A `Legacy sums` @desc The legacy sums in force when the deceased died
      `the net value` IS A NUMBER @desc Net value of the intestate property at the date of death in dollars, leaving out household chattels where a partner survives
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the brothers' and sisters' share` IS
    CONSIDER `Which item of the Table applies` `the family`
    WHEN `item 3, a partner and a parent or sibling line, no issue` THEN
        `the net value` MINUS `the partner's entitlement` `the family` `the sums` `the net value` MINUS `the parents' share` `the family` `the sums` `the net value`
    WHEN `item 6, a parent and a sibling line` THEN `the net value` MINUS `the parents' share` `the family` `the sums` `the net value`
    WHEN `item 8, sibling lines alone` THEN `the net value`
    OTHERWISE 0

§§ `s 14(1) Table, s 14(2b), (3a), s 15 -- how much do I take`

-- Each line of issue takes an equal part (s 14(2b)(b)); each parent an equal share; each brother-and-sister line an equal
-- part (s 14(3a)(b)); grandparents "in equal shares" (item 9); uncle-and-aunt lines as s 14(3a) applies to item 10. The
-- encoder's reading: where no brother or sister survives but the children of two or more dead ones do, s 14(3a)(c)(ii)
-- gives each set of children the part their parent would have had under (b), so the division is still by line.
@export How much do I take from my relative's intestate estate under s 14 and s 15 of the Administration Act 1903 (for a line, the whole line's share)?
GIVEN `who I am` IS A `My place in the family` @desc How you were related to the deceased
      `the family` IS A `The family left behind` @desc Who survived the deceased
      `the sums` IS A `Legacy sums` @desc The legacy sums in force when the deceased died (the original sums unless a s 14A order applies)
      `the net value` IS A NUMBER @desc Net value of the intestate property at the date of death in dollars, leaving out household chattels where a partner survives
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How much do I take` IS
    CONSIDER `who I am`
    WHEN `the surviving husband or wife` THEN
        IF `the family`'s `a husband or wife survived`
        THEN `the partner's entitlement` `the family` `the sums` `the net value` TIMES (1 MINUS `the de facto partner's fraction` `the family`)
        ELSE 0
    WHEN `the de facto partner` THEN
        `the partner's entitlement` `the family` `the sums` `the net value` TIMES `the de facto partner's fraction` `the family`
    WHEN `a child, or the issue of a child who died first, between them` THEN
        `one part of` (`the issue's share` `the family` `the sums` `the net value`) (`the lines of issue` `the family`)
    WHEN `a parent` THEN
        `one part of` (`the parents' share` `the family` `the sums` `the net value`) (`the family`'s `parents who survived`)
    WHEN `a brother or sister, or the children of one who died first, between them` THEN
        `one part of` (`the brothers' and sisters' share` `the family` `the sums` `the net value`) (`the sibling lines` `the family`)
    WHEN `a grandparent` THEN
        IF `Which item of the Table applies` `the family` EQUALS `item 9, grandparents`
        THEN `one part of` `the net value` (`the family`'s `grandparents who survived`)
        ELSE 0
    WHEN `an uncle or aunt, or the children of one who died first, between them` THEN
        IF `Which item of the Table applies` `the family` EQUALS `item 10, uncles and aunts or their children`
        THEN `one part of` `the net value` (`the uncle and aunt lines` `the family`)
        ELSE 0
    OTHERWISE 0
    WHERE
        `one part of` `the whole` `the parts` MEANS
            IF `the parts` EQUALS 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the whole` DIVIDED BY `the parts`

-- Item 1: "the surviving husband or wife shall be entitled, absolutely, to all household chattels"; s 14(2)(a):
-- "household chattels means articles of personal or household use or adornment". s 15 divides them with the rest of the
-- partner's entitlement. Answered as the fraction of the chattels I take.
@export What fraction of the household chattels do I take under Table item 1 (with s 15)?
GIVEN `who I am` IS A `My place in the family` @desc How you were related to the deceased
      `the family` IS A `The family left behind` @desc Who survived the deceased
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What share of the household chattels do I take` IS
    CONSIDER `who I am`
    WHEN `the surviving husband or wife` THEN
        IF `the family`'s `a husband or wife survived` THEN 1 MINUS `the de facto partner's fraction` `the family` ELSE 0
    WHEN `the de facto partner` THEN `the de facto partner's fraction` `the family`
    OTHERWISE 0

§§ `s 12A -- children of parents not married to each other`

-- s 12A(1): "the relationship between a child and his parents shall be determined irrespective of whether the parents are
-- or have been married to each other". s 12A(2)(b): where they are not or have not been married, the relationship is
-- recognised only "(i) if parentage is admitted by or established against the parent in his lifetime; and (ii) where the
-- purpose ... enures for the benefit of the parent, if parentage has been so admitted or established in the lifetime of
-- the child". s 12A(2a): (2)(b) does not apply to a relationship established by the Artificial Conception Act 1985.
-- s 12A(2)(a): the fact must be "established to the reasonable satisfaction of the Court" -- this answer is the Act's
-- rule, if the Court is so satisfied.
@export Is my parent-child relationship with the deceased recognised for intestacy (s 12A), if the Court is satisfied of the facts?
GIVEN `my parents are or have been married to each other` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the child's parents are or ever were married to each other
      `established under the Artificial Conception Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the relationship was established by the Artificial Conception Act 1985
      `parentage admitted or established in the parent's lifetime` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the parent admitted parentage, or it was established against them, while the parent was alive
      `I am the parent claiming from the child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim is a parent's claim on a child's estate
      `parentage admitted or established in the child's lifetime` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether parentage was admitted or established while the child was alive
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my relationship recognised` IF
       `my parents are or have been married to each other`
    OR `established under the Artificial Conception Act`
    OR (    `parentage admitted or established in the parent's lifetime`
        AND (    NOT `I am the parent claiming from the child`
              OR `parentage admitted or established in the child's lifetime`))

§§ `s 13(1a) -- land held for two or more`

-- s 13(1a): "When, pursuant to subsection (1), real estate is held on trust for 2 or more persons, it shall be held as if
-- it had been devised to those persons as tenants in common."
@export Do we who inherit my relative's land on intestacy hold it as tenants in common (s 13(1a))?
GIVEN `the persons entitled to the land` IS A NUMBER @desc How many people are entitled to the land under ss 14 and 15
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do we hold the land as tenants in common` IF
    `the persons entitled to the land` AT LEAST 2

§§ `Fourth Schedule cll 1-3 -- the surviving partner's election to take the home`

-- cl 1(1): where "(b) the whole or a part of the intestate property consists of an interest in a dwelling house that, at
-- the date of the death ... was ordinarily used by the surviving husband or wife as his or her ordinary place of residence"
-- and "(c) the surviving husband or wife is not entitled under sections 14 and 15 to the whole of the intestate property",
-- the surviving husband or wife "may elect to have the interest appropriated ... in or towards satisfaction of" their
-- item 2 or 3 entitlement. cl 1(2): not where the interest is a tenancy that would end, or the landlord could end, within
-- 2 years of the death. cl 2: where the house is part of a building wholly in the estate, held with farm land in the
-- estate, or used in whole or part as a hotel or lodging house or for non-domestic purposes, the right is not exercisable
-- "unless the Court, on being satisfied that the exercise of that right is not likely to diminish the value of assets ...
-- or make them more difficult to dispose of, so orders". cl 3: not "after the expiration of 12 months from the first
-- grant of administration", which "the Court may ... extend". s 15(5): a qualifying de facto partner is taken to be the
-- husband or wife. The Court's judgments are answered conditionally.
DECLARE `The home` HAS
    `the intestate property includes an interest in the house`                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `I ordinarily lived there at the death`                                           IS A BOOLEAN
    `it is a tenancy that would end, or the landlord could end, within 2 years`       IS A BOOLEAN
    `it is part of a building, farm, hotel or lodging house, or partly not domestic`  IS A BOOLEAN   -- cl 2(a)-(d)
    `months since the first grant of administration`                                  IS A NUMBER

DECLARE `My right to take the home` IS ONE OF
    `I may elect to take the home towards my share`
    `only if the Court so orders`
    `only if the Court extends the 12 months`
    `only if the Court so orders and extends the 12 months`
    `I may not elect`

@export As the surviving husband, wife or de facto partner, may I elect to take the home towards my share (Fourth Schedule)?
GIVEN `who I am` IS A `My place in the family` @desc How you were related to the deceased
      `the family` IS A `The family left behind` @desc Who survived the deceased
      `the sums` IS A `Legacy sums` @desc The legacy sums in force when the deceased died
      `the net value` IS A NUMBER @desc Net value of the intestate property at the date of death in dollars, leaving out household chattels
      `the home` IS A `The home` @desc Facts about the house you lived in
GIVETH A `My right to take the home`
DECIDE `May I elect to take the home` IS
    IF     NOT `I take as a partner`
        OR NOT `the home`'s `the intestate property includes an interest in the house`
        OR NOT `the home`'s `I ordinarily lived there at the death`
        OR `How much do I take` `who I am` `the family` `the sums` `the net value` AT LEAST `the net value`
        OR `the home`'s `it is a tenancy that would end, or the landlord could end, within 2 years`
    THEN `I may not elect`
    ELSE IF `the home`'s `it is part of a building, farm, hotel or lodging house, or partly not domestic`
         THEN IF `out of time` THEN `only if the Court so orders and extends the 12 months` ELSE `only if the Court so orders`
         ELSE IF `out of time` THEN `only if the Court extends the 12 months` ELSE `I may elect to take the home towards my share`
    WHERE
        `out of time` MEANS `the home`'s `months since the first grant of administration` GREATER THAN 12
        `I take as a partner` MEANS
            CONSIDER `who I am`
            WHEN `the surviving husband or wife` THEN `the family`'s `a husband or wife survived` AND `the de facto partner's fraction` `the family` LESS THAN 1
            WHEN `the de facto partner` THEN `Does a de facto partner take` `the family`
            OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `ss 6, 7, 25, 33, 36 -- which grant`

-- s 6: the Court may "grant probate of the will or administration of the estate of any deceased person leaving property,
-- whether real or personal, in Western Australia". s 7: "probate to one or more of the executors named in any will".
-- s 33(1): "Where an infant is sole executor, administration with the will annexed may be granted to the guardian".
-- s 36: "Where a person dies leaving a will but without having appointed an executor, or leaving a will and having
-- appointed an executor who is not willing and competent to take probate or is resident out of Western Australia, the
-- Court may appoint an administrator". s 25: administration "of the estate of a person dying intestate".
-- The encoder's reading: s 36 is a power ("may"), so this names the grant the Act points to, not the Court's decision.
DECLARE `Grant` IS ONE OF
    `probate`
    `administration with the will annexed`
    `letters of administration`

DECLARE `The death` HAS
    `left a will`                                    IS A BOOLEAN
    `the will appoints an executor`                  IS A BOOLEAN
    `the executor is an infant`                      IS A BOOLEAN   -- under 18 (s 33)
    `the executor is willing and competent`          IS A BOOLEAN
    `the executor resides in Western Australia`      IS A BOOLEAN

@export Which grant must be applied for to deal with my relative's estate in Western Australia (probate, administration with the will annexed, or letters of administration)?
GIVEN `the death` IS A `The death` @desc Whether there is a will and an executor able to act
GIVETH A `Grant`
DECIDE `Which grant does the estate need` IS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the death`'s `left a will`                       THEN `letters of administration`
        IF NOT `the death`'s `the will appoints an executor`     THEN `administration with the will annexed`
        IF `the death`'s `the executor is an infant`             THEN `administration with the will annexed`
        IF     `the death`'s `the executor is willing and competent`
           AND `the death`'s `the executor resides in Western Australia`
                                                                 THEN `probate`
        OTHERWISE                                                     `administration with the will annexed`

§§ `ss 6, 8 -- what the grant carries`

-- s 8: "Upon the grant of probate or administration, all real and personal estate which a deceased person dies seised,
-- possessed of, or entitled to in Western Australia shall, as from the death of such person, pass to and become vested in
-- the executor ... or administrator". Whether an asset passed outside the estate (to a surviving joint tenant, or under a
-- superannuation nomination) is decided by other law and asked as a fact.
@export Does the grant vest this asset of the deceased in me as executor or administrator (s 8)?
GIVEN `the asset is in Western Australia` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the asset is situated in Western Australia
      `the asset passed outside the estate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether other law passed it directly to someone at the death (survivorship of a joint owner, a binding super nomination)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the grant vest this asset in me` IF
    `the asset is in Western Australia` AND NOT `the asset passed outside the estate`

§§ `s 25 -- who may be granted administration on intestacy`

-- s 25(1): the Court may grant administration "to the following persons (separately or conjointly) being of the full age
-- of 18 years ... (a) one or more of the persons entitled in distribution to the estate of the intestate; (b) any other
-- person, whether a creditor or not, if there be no such person entitled as aforesaid resident within the jurisdiction
-- and fit to be so entrusted, or if the person entitled as aforesaid fails, when duly cited, to appear and apply".
-- s 3: "administrator includes the Public Trustee". The Public Trustee Act 1941 is a separate route, not encoded.
@export Am I someone the Court may grant administration of an intestate estate to (s 25)?
GIVEN `my age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in years
      `I am entitled in distribution` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you take a share of the estate under s 14 or s 15
      `someone entitled is resident in WA and fit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether at least one person entitled to a share lives in Western Australia and is fit to be entrusted
      `the entitled person failed to appear when cited` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person entitled was duly cited and failed to appear and apply
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I be granted administration` IF
        `my age` AT LEAST 18
    AND (    `I am entitled in distribution`
          OR NOT `someone entitled is resident in WA and fit`
          OR `the entitled person failed to appear when cited`)

@export May someone not entitled to a share, such as the Public Trustee or a creditor, be granted administration of my relative's intestate estate under s 25(1)(b)?
GIVEN `someone entitled is resident in WA and fit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether at least one person entitled to a share lives in Western Australia and is fit to be entrusted
      `the entitled person failed to appear when cited` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person entitled was duly cited and failed to appear and apply
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May an outsider be granted administration` IF
    NOT `someone entitled is resident in WA and fit` OR `the entitled person failed to appear when cited`

§§ `ss 32, 37 -- losing the executor's place`

-- s 32: "Where an executor renounces probate of the will, or dies without having taken probate, or where, being personally
-- cited to take probate, he does not appear to such citation, the right of such executor in respect of the executorship
-- shall wholly cease".
@export As the executor named in the will, have I lost my right to the executorship (s 32)?
GIVEN `I renounced probate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you renounced probate of the will
      `I was personally cited and did not appear` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were personally cited to take probate and did not appear
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Have I lost my right to be executor` IF
    `I renounced probate` OR `I was personally cited and did not appear`

-- s 37: "Where an executor neglects to obtain or to renounce probate within 2 months from the death of the testator or
-- from the time of such executor attaining the age of 18 years ... the Court may, upon the application of any person
-- interested in the estate, or of any creditor of the testator, grant administration with the will annexed to the
-- applicant". The encoder's reading: the 2 months run from the death, or for an executor under 18 at the death, from
-- the executor's 18th birthday.
@export As executor, can the Court now give administration to someone else because I have neither taken out nor renounced probate within 2 months (s 37)?
GIVEN `I have obtained or renounced probate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already obtained or renounced probate
      `I was under 18 at the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were under 18 when the testator died
      `whole months since the death` IS A NUMBER @desc Whole months since the testator died
      `whole months since I turned 18` IS A NUMBER @desc Whole months since your 18th birthday (if you were under 18 at the death)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the Court give administration to someone else for my delay` IF
        NOT `I have obtained or renounced probate`
    AND (IF `I was under 18 at the death` THEN `whole months since I turned 18` AT LEAST 2 ELSE `whole months since the death` AT LEAST 2)

§§ `ss 43(1)(b), 44 -- inventory and accounts`

-- s 43(1): "Every person to whom probate or administration is granted shall be under a duty to ... (b) file an inventory
-- of the estate of the deceased, and pass his accounts relating thereto within such time ... as may be prescribed by the
-- rules or as the Court may order". The rules are not deposited, so the period is an input.
DECLARE `Person administering` IS ONE OF `the executor or administrator`
DECLARE `Step in the administration` IS ONE OF `file the inventory and pass the accounts`

-- @export What is my duty as executor or administrator to file an inventory and pass accounts within the period the rules fix (s 43(1)(b))?
GIVEN `the days the rules allow` IS A NUMBER @desc Days the probate rules (or the Court) allow for filing the inventory and passing accounts
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Person administering` `Step in the administration`
DECIDE `my duty to file the inventory and accounts` IS
    PARTY  `the executor or administrator`
    MUST   `file the inventory and pass the accounts`
    WITHIN `the days the rules allow`
    HENCE  FULFILLED
    LEST   BREACH BY `the executor or administrator`

-- s 44(1): "If an executor or administrator neglects to file an inventory or to pass accounts within one month after the
-- expiration of the period fixed by the rules, the Principal Registrar shall cause such executor or administrator to be
-- notified of such neglect." (2): "In case of further neglect of the period of one month, the Principal Registrar shall
-- apply for an order upon such executor or administrator to file such inventory or exhibit such account forthwith."
DECLARE `What s 44 does to me` IS ONE OF
    `nothing, I have filed`
    `nothing yet`
    `the Principal Registrar must have me notified of the neglect`
    `the Principal Registrar must apply for an order that I file forthwith`

@export As executor or administrator who has not filed the inventory and accounts, what must the Principal Registrar now do about it (s 44)?
GIVEN `I have filed the inventory and passed the accounts` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have filed the inventory and passed the accounts
      `whole months since the period fixed by the rules ended` IS A NUMBER @desc Whole months since the period the rules allow for filing expired (0 if not yet expired)
GIVETH A `What s 44 does to me`
DECIDE `What happens if I have not filed my accounts` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `I have filed the inventory and passed the accounts`                    THEN `nothing, I have filed`
        IF `whole months since the period fixed by the rules ended` LESS THAN 1   THEN `nothing yet`
        IF `whole months since the period fixed by the rules ended` LESS THAN 2   THEN `the Principal Registrar must have me notified of the neglect`
        OTHERWISE                                                                       `the Principal Registrar must apply for an order that I file forthwith`

§§ `s 42 -- a beneficiary whose gift is held back`

-- s 42: "If an executor who has obtained probate, or an administrator with the will annexed, after request in writing
-- neglects or refuses to -- (a) execute a transfer or conveyance of land devised to a devisee; or (b) transfer, pay, or
-- deliver to the person entitled any bequests, legacy, or residuary bequest, such devisee or person may apply for an
-- order upon such executor or administrator to comply with such request".
@export As a beneficiary under the will, may I apply to the Court for an order making the executor hand over my gift (s 42)?
GIVEN `the executor has probate or is administrator with the will annexed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person holding the estate has obtained probate or administration with the will annexed
      `I asked in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked them in writing to transfer, pay or deliver the gift
      `they neglected or refused` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they neglected or refused to do so after the request
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I apply for an order to get my gift` IF
    `the executor has probate or is administrator with the will annexed` AND `I asked in writing` AND `they neglected or refused`

-- s 143A: "Interest payable on legacies shall be computed after the rate of 5% per annum unless any other rate of interest
-- is directed by the will or under a judgment or order of a Court".
@export At what yearly rate is interest on my legacy computed (s 143A)?
GIVEN `the rate the will or the Court directs` IS A MAYBE NUMBER @desc The yearly rate (as a fraction, e.g. 0.03) directed by the will or a Court, if any
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `At what rate is interest on my legacy` IS
    CONSIDER `the rate the will or the Court directs`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN 5 DIVIDED BY 100
    WHEN JUST `the rate` THEN `the rate`

§§ `s 55 -- small estates`

-- s 55: "In all cases where a person dies leaving property not exceeding $10 000 in value, application for probate or
-- administration may be made direct to the Principal Registrar or to a district agent".
@export May I apply for the grant direct to the Principal Registrar or a district agent, because the estate is not over $10,000 (s 55)?
GIVEN `the value of the property` IS A NUMBER @desc Value of all the property the deceased left, in dollars
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I apply direct to the Principal Registrar` IF
    `the value of the property` AT MOST 10000

§§ `s 139 -- bank deposits paid without a grant`

-- s 139(1): a sum "not exceeding the amount of $1 200, or such other amount as may for the time being be declared by
-- proclamation" standing to the deceased's credit in an ADI, "if no probate or administration is produced to that ADI
-- within one month of the death ... and no notice in writing of any will and of intention to prove it or of an intention
-- to apply for administration is given to the ADI within that period", the ADI "may apply" to funeral expenses and the
-- balance to "any person who appears to the satisfaction of the manager of the ADI to be the widower, widow, parent or
-- child of the deceased person or a person who was living as a de facto partner of the deceased person immediately before
-- the deceased person's death". The ceiling is an input (note 4 of the deposit: "now declared to be $50 000"). The
-- manager's satisfaction is answered conditionally: the relation is the one the manager is satisfied of.
DECLARE `My relation to the account holder` IS ONE OF
    `widow or widower`
    `parent`
    `child`
    `de facto partner living with them at the death`
    `brother or sister`
    `someone else`

@export May the bank pay me my relative's deposit without any grant of probate or administration (s 139), if the manager is satisfied of my relationship?
GIVEN `the balance` IS A NUMBER @desc The sum standing to the deceased's credit in the bank or other ADI, in dollars
      `the proclaimed ceiling` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount declared by proclamation (the deposit's note gives $50,000; the Act's text $1,200)
      `a month has passed since the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether one month has passed since the death
      `a grant was produced to the bank within the month` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether probate or administration was produced to the bank within that month
      `notice of a will or an application was given within the month` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the bank was given written notice of a will to be proved or of an intended application for administration within that month
      `my relation` IS A `My relation to the account holder` @desc How you were related to the deceased
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the bank pay me without a grant` IF
        `the balance` AT MOST `the proclaimed ceiling`
    AND `a month has passed since the death`
    AND NOT `a grant was produced to the bank within the month`
    AND NOT `notice of a will or an application was given within the month`
    AND `a relation s 139 names`
    WHERE
        `a relation s 139 names` MEANS
            CONSIDER `my relation`
            WHEN `widow or widower` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `parent` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `child` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `de facto partner living with them at the death` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `s 71, Part V -- the will in evidence, and no death duty`

-- s 71: "No will or codicil of any deceased person shall be registered or receivable or admissible in evidence, except in
-- criminal proceedings, or upon application for probate or letters of administration, or in proceedings relating to the
-- validity of any such will or codicil, or in proceedings under this Part, until probate or letters of administration in
-- respect of the estate comprised therein shall have been issued or obtained."
DECLARE `Proceeding` IS ONE OF
    `criminal proceedings`
    `an application for a grant`
    `proceedings on the validity of the will`
    `any other proceedings`

@export Can my relative's will be put in evidence in these proceedings before a grant has issued (s 71)?
GIVEN `the proceeding` IS A `Proceeding` @desc The kind of proceeding the will is to be used in
      `a grant has issued` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether probate or administration has already issued
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the will admissible in evidence` IF
    `a grant has issued`
    OR (CONSIDER `the proceeding`
        WHEN `any other proceedings` THEN FALSE
        OTHERWISE TRUE)

-- Part V is headed "Duties on deceased persons' estates and succession duties", but every charging section (ss 65-70A,
-- 72-137) is "Deleted: No. 80 of 1973 s. 3"; only s 71 remains. This Act charges no duty on an estate.
@export Must my relative's estate pay death or succession duty under the Administration Act 1903?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the estate pay death duty under this Act` IS FALSE
